// AUTO-GENERATED deep overlay for JEMAS(PG) 2025 CP — concept + per-distractor traps,
// index-matched to data-all.js (Q15-28, Q59-66, Q75-88 = 36 items). Verbatim from the booklet scans.
window.PREVIEW_DEEP = Object.assign(window.PREVIEW_DEEP||{}, {"jemas-2025": [{"q": "Which of the following is a mature defense mechanism?", "concept": "The correct answer is: Altruism. Altruism. George Vaillant ranked the ego defences from pathological up to mature. The MATURE tier (the healthiest, most adaptive) is altruism, sublimation, humour, suppression and anticipation. Altruism channels one's own needs into genuinely helpful service to others, so it sits at the top of the ladder. The other three choices are all lower, neurotic-level defences.", "traps": {"b": "Intellectualisation (handling conflict in abstract, emotionless terms) is a neurotic defence, not mature.", "c": "Repression (automatically pushing unacceptable material out of awareness) is a neurotic-tier defence.", "d": "Rationalisation (self-justifying but false reasons for one's behaviour) is a neurotic defence."}}, {"q": "The basic assumption of Adler's individual psychology is", "concept": "The correct answer is: human behaviour is purposeful and goal directed.. Behaviour is purposeful and goal directed. This teleology is the core of Adler: people are pulled by future goals (striving for superiority, fictional finalism) rather than only pushed by past drives. Optimism (b) and social embeddedness/social interest (c) are Adlerian flavours but not the defining basic assumption, and (d) is Erikson's lifespan/ego-identity view.", "traps": {"b": "An optimistic view is a feature of Adler's tone, not the foundational assumption of the theory.", "c": "Social interest matters to Adler, but the BASIC assumption is the goal-directed, purposive nature of behaviour.", "d": "Lifelong development with ego identity central is Erik Erikson's model, not Adler's."}}, {"q": "Biologically based characteristic way of reacting is known as", "concept": "The correct answer is: Temperament. Temperament: the biologically based, inborn and relatively stable style of emotional and behavioural reactivity, visible from early infancy (Thomas & Chess easy/difficult/slow-to-warm-up). Character is the learned, value-laden part shaped by socialisation; a trait is a broader descriptive dimension of individual difference; disposition is too loose a term.", "traps": {"a": "Disposition is a vague everyday term, not the technical construct for the innate reactive style.", "b": "Character is the learned, moral/value-laden component of personality, not the biological baseline.", "c": "A trait is a descriptive dimension of personality, but 'biologically based inborn reactivity' points specifically to temperament."}}, {"q": "Trait approaches to personality focuses on", "concept": "The correct answer is: specific psychological attributes along which individuals tend to differ in consistent and stable way.. The trait tradition (Allport, Cattell, Eysenck, the Big Five) defines a trait as a specific, enduring attribute along which people differ consistently (across situations) and stably (across time). Option (b) states exactly that. (d) is the situationist critique of trait theory, the opposite emphasis.", "traps": {"a": "'Broad patterns' is closer to a type approach; the trait approach targets specific measurable dimensions of difference.", "c": "Communicating expected behaviour from similarities is not the trait definition.", "d": "Stressing situational determinants is the situationist position that opposes trait theory."}}, {"q": "Which area is known as the satiety centre?", "concept": "The correct answer is: Ventromedial hypothalamus. The ventromedial hypothalamus (VMH) is the satiety centre: stimulating it stops eating, lesioning it causes hyperphagia and obesity (VMH syndrome). The lateral hypothalamus is the opposite, the hunger/feeding centre. Mnemonic: Ventromedial = Very Much full.", "traps": {"a": "The lateral hypothalamus is the HUNGER centre; lesioning it causes aphagia, the reverse of satiety.", "c": "The cerebrum is the higher cortical mantle, not a feeding centre.", "d": "The basal ganglia govern movement and procedural learning, not satiety."}}, {"q": "A universal hypothesis is one in which", "concept": "The correct answer is: the stated relationship holds good for all the values or levels of variables which are specified for all times at all places.. A universal hypothesis makes an all-encompassing claim: the stated relationship holds for all values/levels of the variables, at all times and in all places (no exceptions). The other options describe existential or particular hypotheses that hold only for one, two, or some cases.", "traps": {"b": "Holding for at least one case is an existential hypothesis, the opposite of universal.", "c": "Holding for two levels is a limited, particular claim.", "d": "Holding for 'some' values is again particular, not universal."}}, {"q": "NEO-PI personality is based on", "concept": "The correct answer is: Trait Approach. The NEO-PI (Costa & McCrae) measures the Big Five / Five-Factor Model, so it belongs squarely to the trait approach to personality.", "traps": {"a": "Behaviourism rejects internal traits and focuses on observable stimulus-response learning.", "b": "The cognitive approach studies mental processes, not the NEO's trait dimensions.", "c": "The psychodynamic approach centres on unconscious conflict, not measured trait factors."}}, {"q": "Among the following which one is not a projective test?", "concept": "The correct answer is: Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory. The MMPI is an OBJECTIVE self-report inventory with fixed true/false items and standardised scoring, not a projective test. The Somatic Inkblot Series, TAT and Rorschach all use ambiguous stimuli onto which the person projects, so they are projective.", "traps": {"b": "The Somatic Inkblot Series uses ambiguous inkblots, making it projective.", "c": "The TAT asks the person to project stories onto ambiguous pictures, a classic projective test.", "d": "The Rorschach is the prototypical projective inkblot test."}}, {"q": "Location in Rorschach Inkblot Test is concerned with", "concept": "The correct answer is: Whole/Part. In Rorschach scoring, Location codes WHERE in the blot the response was seen: the whole blot (W), a common detail (D), an unusual detail (Dd) or white space (S). So Location is the whole-versus-part dimension. Colour and Movement are Determinants, not Location.", "traps": {"b": "Fantasy/emotion is thematic content, not the Location code.", "c": "Colour and Movement are Determinants (how the percept was formed), a different scoring category.", "d": "Figure/Landscape is not a Rorschach location category."}}, {"q": "A syndrome is", "concept": "The correct answer is: a symptom cluster.. A syndrome is a cluster of signs and symptoms that regularly occur together and characterise a particular condition. It is not a single sign such as a murmur or a hearing loss.", "traps": {"a": "A free radical is a reactive molecule in biochemistry, unrelated to the definition.", "b": "A hearing impairment is one specific deficit, not a cluster of co-occurring symptoms.", "c": "A heart murmur is a single clinical sign, whereas a syndrome is a set of them together."}}, {"q": "Someone diagnosed as suffering from Agoraphobia would be", "concept": "The correct answer is: feeling an irrational fear of public places.. Agoraphobia is marked fear/anxiety about public or open situations where escape may be difficult (crowds, open spaces, public transport, being outside the home alone). Option (d) captures that. Hearing voices is a psychotic symptom, not a phobia.", "traps": {"a": "Hearing command voices is an auditory hallucination (psychosis), not agoraphobia.", "b": "Fear specifically of enclosed spaces like an elevator is claustrophobia, the near-opposite.", "c": "A specific fear of trees or buildings is a specific phobia, not the fear of public/open situations."}}, {"q": "Identify the following statements that least accurately characterizes depression.", "concept": "The correct answer is: Men are more vulnerable to depression than women.. The LEAST accurate statement is (c). Epidemiologically, women are roughly twice as likely as men to be diagnosed with major depression, so 'men are more vulnerable' is false. The other three statements are accurate.", "traps": {"a": "Depression is indeed widespread and rising, so this statement is accurate (not the answer).", "b": "Stressful life events commonly precipitate depressive episodes, an accurate statement.", "d": "Greater depression severity does raise suicide risk, an accurate statement."}}, {"q": "Symptoms where a person believes that other people are out to get them and that he/she is being attacked by rays from outer space. These patients are experiencing", "concept": "The correct answer is: Paranoia.. Fixed false beliefs of persecution ('people are out to get me', 'attacked by rays') are persecutory delusions, the hallmark of paranoia (paranoid ideation seen in paranoid schizophrenia / delusional disorder).", "traps": {"a": "Depression centres on low mood and anhedonia, not persecutory delusions.", "c": "Dissociative disorders involve disruptions of memory/identity/consciousness, not persecutory beliefs.", "d": "Anxiety disorders involve excessive fear/worry, not fixed delusions of persecution."}}, {"q": "To describe a predisposition towards and risk triggers for a mental disorder, modern theorists use a", "concept": "The correct answer is: Diathesis – Stress model.. The diathesis-stress model explains disorder as the interaction of a pre-existing vulnerability (diathesis: genetic, biological or psychological predisposition) with environmental stress (the risk trigger). That predisposition-plus-trigger framing is exactly what the item describes.", "traps": {"b": "The neurodevelopmental model attributes disorder to early brain development, not the vulnerability-plus-stressor interaction.", "c": "There is no standard 'clinical-reductionist' model of this kind.", "d": "The syndrome-sign model concerns how disorders are classified by symptoms, not predisposition and triggers."}}, {"q": "According to DSM-5, which of the following is required for a diagnosis of Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD)?", "concept": "The correct answer is: Persistent deficits in social communication and interaction across multiple contexts. DSM-5 requires persistent deficits in social communication and social interaction across multiple contexts, PLUS restricted, repetitive patterns of behaviour/interests. Option (b) states the first core criterion. Language delay, intellectual disability and hyperactivity are neither necessary nor sufficient.", "traps": {"a": "Delayed speech alone is neither required nor sufficient; DSM-5 dropped language delay as a core criterion.", "c": "Intellectual disability may co-occur but is not required for ASD.", "d": "Hyperactivity and impulsivity describe ADHD, not the defining ASD criteria."}}, {"q": "The DSM-5 criteria for specific learning disorder include", "concept": "The correct answer is: Average intellectual functioning with persistent difficulties in academic skills. Specific Learning Disorder in DSM-5 is persistent difficulty in a specific academic skill (reading, writing or maths) that is substantially below age expectation despite otherwise adequate (roughly average) intelligence and schooling. Option (a) captures that unexpected-underachievement picture.", "traps": {"b": "An IQ below 50 indicates intellectual disability, not a specific learning disorder.", "c": "An attention deficit alone points to ADHD, not SLD.", "d": "An isolated motor coordination deficit is Developmental Coordination Disorder, not SLD."}}, {"q": "Brain regions in procedural learning is related to", "concept": "The correct answer is: cerebellum, basal ganglia, motor cortex. Procedural (implicit, skill) learning depends on the cerebellum, basal ganglia and motor cortex. The hippocampus, in contrast, supports declarative/explicit memory, which is why option (d) is the trap.", "traps": {"a": "The hindbrain and spinal cord handle basic reflexes and life support, not skill learning.", "c": "The frontal/association cortex supports executive and higher associative functions, not the core motor-skill circuit.", "d": "The hippocampus and amygdala serve declarative and emotional memory, not procedural skill learning."}}, {"q": "Freudian slip are analyzed in", "concept": "The correct answer is: Free association. Parapraxes (Freudian slips) are treated as products of the unconscious and are explored through free association, the core psychoanalytic technique in which the patient reports whatever comes to mind so latent meaning can surface.", "traps": {"b": "Behaviour analysis studies observable behaviour and reinforcement, ignoring unconscious slips.", "c": "Case studies are a research format, not the interpretive technique for slips.", "d": "Sleep study/dream analysis targets dreams; slips are worked through via free association."}}, {"q": "Which is not attained in Piaget's concrete operational stage of cognitive development?", "concept": "The correct answer is: Hypothetico deductive reasoning. Hypothetico-deductive reasoning (systematic, abstract 'if-then' hypothesis testing) is achieved in the FORMAL operational stage, not the concrete operational stage. Reduced egocentrism and transitive inference are concrete-operational gains, and deferred imitation appears even earlier (sensorimotor).", "traps": {"a": "Decline of egocentrism is a hallmark of the concrete operational stage, so it IS attained here.", "b": "Deferred imitation emerges by the end of the sensorimotor stage, so it is already present.", "d": "Transitive inference (A>B, B>C, so A>C) is a concrete-operational achievement."}}, {"q": "Language development is compromised most commonly in", "concept": "The correct answer is: Autism spectrum disorder.. Language and communication impairment is a defining, near-universal feature of Autism Spectrum Disorder, so language development is most commonly compromised there. (Note: this is a key-debate item; some keys mark specific learning disability, so verify against the official JEMAS key.)", "traps": {"a": "ADHD primarily affects attention and impulse control; language may lag but is not a core deficit.", "c": "Specific learning disability targets a specific academic skill (often reading), not global language development.", "d": "Childhood-onset dissociation is rare and not characterised by language delay."}}, {"q": "A person's language and thought involve", "concept": "The correct answer is: Simultaneous processes.. ⚠ Key-debate item (no official key on hand). Language and thought are treated here as simultaneous, interwoven processes that operate together in verbal thinking (Vygotsky's convergence of thought and speech). Verify against the official JEMAS key, since 'shared processes' is also defensible.", "traps": {"b": "'Independent processes' reflects the older view that thought and language are wholly separate, which most modern accounts reject.", "c": "'Shared processes' is close in meaning and is the main alternative answer to check against the official key.", "d": "'Alternate process' (one then the other) does not fit the integrated view of verbal thought."}}, {"q": "Primacy and recency effects in psychophysics experiment are controlled by", "concept": "The correct answer is: counter balancing. Primacy and recency are order (sequence) effects. In psychophysics the systematic influence of trial order is controlled by counterbalancing, presenting stimulus orders in a balanced, reversed way (e.g. ascending/descending series) so order effects cancel out.", "traps": {"b": "Randomisation reduces bias generally but does not systematically balance out primacy/recency order effects the way counterbalancing does.", "c": "Pairing/matching controls subject variables, not order effects.", "d": "The double-blind technique controls experimenter/participant bias, not order effects."}}, {"q": "To determine whether a newly developed intelligence test truly accurately measures intelligence or not, it is necessary to administer the test with one that has been in the field for some time. Which of the following needs to be looked for?", "concept": "The correct answer is: Concurrent validity. Correlating a new test with an already established test given at the same time is CONCURRENT validity (a form of criterion validity where the criterion is measured concurrently). The presence of an existing well-validated test administered alongside is the giveaway.", "traps": {"a": "Construct validity is the broader question of whether the test measures the theoretical construct; here the method is specifically comparison with an existing test.", "c": "Content validity concerns whether items cover the domain, judged by experts, not comparison with another test.", "d": "Predictive validity checks correlation with a FUTURE criterion, not a test given concurrently."}}, {"q": "If the mean, median and mode are indentical, its a", "concept": "The correct answer is: Normal distribution. When mean = median = mode and they all coincide at the centre, the distribution is symmetrical, the normal (bell) curve. In skewed distributions the three measures separate; a bimodal distribution has two peaks.", "traps": {"b": "In a negatively skewed distribution mean < median < mode, so they are not identical.", "c": "In a positively skewed distribution mean > median > mode, so they differ.", "d": "A bimodal distribution has two modes and the measures do not coincide."}}, {"q": "A person wants to determine the representative income of the people living in a community. He would be best advised to use the", "concept": "The correct answer is: Median. Income distributions are strongly positively skewed by a few very high earners, which drags the mean upward. The median (the middle value) is unaffected by those extreme outliers and is the more representative measure of typical income.", "traps": {"a": "The mean is pulled up by a few very high incomes, over-stating the 'typical' income.", "c": "The mode is just the most frequent income and can be unstable or unrepresentative.", "d": "The standard deviation measures spread, not the central/representative value."}}, {"q": "Z-scores", "concept": "The correct answer is: Enable researchers and students to compare results from different distributions.. A z-score restates a raw score in standard-deviation units from its own mean, putting scores from different distributions onto a common scale so they can be directly compared. That comparability across distributions is its main purpose.", "traps": {"b": "Variability is measured by the variance/standard deviation, not by an individual z-score.", "c": "The semi-interquartile range is a separate dispersion measure.", "d": "Z-scores standardise individual scores; they do not describe central tendency."}}, {"q": "Learned helplessness phenomenon is offered by", "concept": "The correct answer is: Martin Seligmam and Steven F. Maier.. Learned helplessness was demonstrated by Martin Seligman and Steven Maier (dogs exposed to inescapable shock later failed to escape avoidable shock). It became an influential model of depression.", "traps": {"a": "Aaron T. Beck developed the cognitive theory/triad of depression, not learned helplessness.", "c": "Harry Stack Sullivan founded interpersonal psychiatry.", "d": "Daniel Kahneman is known for heuristics, biases and prospect theory."}}, {"q": "A score is considered to be statistically significant if", "concept": "The correct answer is: it is more than two standard deviation from the mean.. By the usual .05 convention, a value is statistically significant (unlikely to be due to chance) when it lies beyond about two standard deviations from the mean (roughly the outer 5%). So 'more than two SD from the mean' is the significance threshold.", "traps": {"a": "About 68% of scores fall within one SD; being one SD out is common, not significant.", "c": "Within one SD of the mean is the most typical region, clearly not significant.", "d": "Between one and two SD is still within the common range and does not reach the usual significance cut-off."}}, {"q": "In which disorder there is a change in the awareness of one's own identity and patient feel that he is no longer his normal natural self?", "concept": "The correct answer is: Depersonalisation. Depersonalisation is the sense of being detached from, or an unreal observer of, one's own self ('I am not my normal natural self'). It is contrasted with derealisation, where it is the outside world that feels unreal.", "traps": {"a": "Derealisation is a change in the sense of the EXTERNAL world's reality, not of one's own self.", "c": "Stress is a general response state, not this specific identity-detachment experience.", "d": "Depression is a mood disorder, not the detachment-from-self phenomenon."}}, {"q": "\"Hallucination\" is a disorder of", "concept": "The correct answer is: Perception. A hallucination is a perception without an external stimulus, so it is a disorder of PERCEPTION. Contrast it with a delusion, which is a disorder of thought/belief.", "traps": {"a": "Impaired judgement concerns decision-making, not false perceptions.", "c": "Intelligence deficits are about cognitive ability, not perception.", "d": "A disorder of thinking is a delusion; a hallucination is specifically perceptual."}}, {"q": "Which type of intelligence according to Sternberg's Triarchic theory of intelligence includes the ability to easily adopt to the present environment or select a more favourable environment than the existing one or modify the environment to fit one's need?", "concept": "The correct answer is: Contextual. Sternberg's CONTEXTUAL (practical) intelligence is the ability to adapt to, shape, or select environments, exactly the adapt/select/modify language in the stem. Componential is analytical intelligence; experiential is the third (creative) component.", "traps": {"a": "Componential (analytical) intelligence is about internal information processing, not adapting to the environment.", "c": "'Incremental' is not one of Sternberg's three intelligences (it echoes Dweck's mindset theory).", "d": "Sternberg's third component is experiential/creative, not 'experimental'; and it is not the adapt/select/shape ability."}}, {"q": "\"First rank symptoms\" of schizophrenia given by Schneider include all except", "concept": "The correct answer is: Depersonalization. Schneider's first-rank symptoms include audible thoughts, voices arguing or giving a running commentary, thought withdrawal/insertion/broadcasting, delusional perception, and passivity/made experiences. Depersonalisation is NOT a first-rank symptom, so it is the exception.", "traps": {"b": "A running-commentary hallucinatory voice is a classic Schneiderian first-rank symptom.", "c": "Delusional perception is one of Schneider's first-rank symptoms.", "d": "Thought broadcasting is a first-rank symptom, so it is not the exception."}}, {"q": "'Nihilistic delusions' are commonly seen in", "concept": "The correct answer is: Depression.. Nihilistic delusions (the belief that oneself, one's organs, or the world do not exist or are dead/decaying, as in Cotard's syndrome) are most characteristically seen in severe (psychotic) depression.", "traps": {"b": "Schizophrenia features many delusions but nihilistic delusions are classically tied to severe depression.", "c": "OCD involves obsessions and compulsions with insight, not fixed nihilistic delusions.", "d": "Somatoform disorders involve bodily symptoms without delusional denial of existence."}}, {"q": "The concept of object permanence is attained in which stage of cognitive development?", "concept": "The correct answer is: Sensory motor. Object permanence, knowing an object still exists when out of sight, develops during Piaget's SENSORIMOTOR stage (roughly 0-2 years, consolidating around 8-12 months).", "traps": {"b": "The formal operational stage (11+) brings abstract reasoning, long after object permanence.", "c": "The concrete operational stage (7-11) brings conservation and logical operations, not the first grasp of object permanence.", "d": "The pre-operational stage (2-7) follows the sensorimotor stage; object permanence is already established by then."}}, {"q": "What is 'difficult child syndrome'?", "concept": "The correct answer is: Some infants are difficult even in the first few weeks after their birth. In Thomas and Chess's temperament research, the 'difficult child' is an infant who from the first weeks of life shows irregular routines, intense negative reactions and slow adaptation to change, i.e. a temperament evident very early after birth.", "traps": {"b": "Behavioural problems appearing in late childhood are not the early-emerging temperament pattern described.", "c": "Turning the head to a sound is an orienting/auditory reflex, not the difficult-temperament construct.", "d": "Failure to develop voluntary control is a motor/developmental issue, not difficult temperament."}}, {"q": "Principle of identity in early childhood refers to", "concept": "The correct answer is: the ability to recognize that certain characteristics of objects remain fixed even when the appearance of the object changes.. The principle (or concept) of identity is the child's understanding that an object's essential nature stays the same even when its surface appearance changes, a precursor to conservation (e.g. the same amount of water despite a differently shaped glass).", "traps": {"a": "Recognising positive traits about oneself is self-esteem, not the identity principle.", "c": "Identifying with one's gender is gender identity, a different concept.", "d": "Bringing behaviour into harmony with the world is social adaptation/regulation, not object identity."}}]});
